# ---- excluded, each with its reason -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# test/
#     Not shipped. The tests are the other half of the method rather than a subject of it: where a
#     property can be asserted a test beats a finding, and a test that was wrong would show up as a
#     finding against the code it guards.
#
# tools/get.ts, tools/serve.ts, tools/verify.sh, tools/verify-browser.ts
#     (`tools/inline.ts` is in scope, not here: it decides what the shipped page's script element
#     contains, which is the build's one injection site.)
#     Never reach a visitor. `get.ts` and `verify.sh` are the gateway's own instruments, `serve.ts`
#     is the local static host, and none of the four is in the deployable: the build writes three
#     files and `tools/build.ts` fails on a fourth. A bug in them costs a wrong measurement, which
#     is a real cost, and not an attack on anybody's browser.
#
# chains.json
#     Data a deployer replaces. Which endpoints are believed is a deployment decision; what the code
#     does with a hostile or lying endpoint is in scope, and `src/adapters/rpc/fetch.ts` is where it
#     is decided.
#
# devbox.json, devbox.lock, package.json, package-lock.json, mk/, .github/
#     The toolchain and the gate, not the deployable. `tools/build.ts` is in scope because it is the
#     program that composes what ships, including the values it substitutes; the build's own
#     dependencies are outside the review and README.md § What the review did not cover says so.
#
# ---- one file in scope that does not ship -------------------------------------------------------
#
# src/adapters/rpc/memory.ts is in this list because the scope rule is every file under
# `src/adapters/rpc/`, and it is kept rather than argued away. Measured at the review commit: only
# `test/resolve.test.ts` imports it, and `grep -c memory` on the built `gw44-sw.js` is 0, so it is
# not in the deployable. It defines the `Rpc` contract every other adapter meets, which is worth a
# read even though a finding in it cannot reach a visitor by itself.
